This PR contains support for local variables. It allows shadowing a variable from outer scope, but not in the same scope. This is valid

```Ruby
var name="Ameer";
{
    var name="Hamza";
}
```
but the following code isn't valid

```Ruby
{
    var name="Ameer";
    var name="Hamza";
}
```

Notice that I used local variables to show that shadowing isn't allowed like this. Because in global scope, shadowing is allowed for REPL.

There's also one case which is not allowed. Consider this code:
```Ruby
var name="Hamza";
{
    var name=name;
}
```

Above code should throw an error because local variable `name` has been declared before evaluating the expression, which is reading the `name` variable. Variable declaration should be marked as un-initialized first. Assigning/reading an un-initialized variable while declaration should throw the following error:
```bash
Compiler Error: [line [line_number]] Error at '[var_name]': Can't read local variable in its own initializer
```
